Z||000017||Slammed Fiero||12-18-2001||02:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LAMBO:<BR><B>MinnGreen, I may be up in Eagan over New Years visiting relatives. I might give your Dad a call when I'm up there to see what he has. <P>Slammed, if you read this, do you have any info on your 215 powered 'vair. I'd sure like to know more about this. Did you make your own adapter plate, etc. <P>TL</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The only part designed for the conversion was the Bell housing from kennedy Engineering and an ISky Marine grind cam.<P>Since the engine sits behind the transaxle the motor has to run backwards. This means reverse grind cam and cam gears. Your firing order is backwards , as well keep in mind the effect on all the thrust surfaces. Needless to say it's quite an undertaking. We built engine mounts , and had to slightly notch the frame. As well engineered the entire cooling system. This car was Finished in 1983. The z28 Wheels you see on it actually were ont he car before the Z28 ever hit showrooms thanks to a friend at GM.<P>The nice part is it weights the same as the Corvair Flat 6. Total additional weight gain was only 67lbs. The car tips the scales at 2510 Lbs. The engine shares a lot of the same architechture with a Buick v6. we used buick V6 headers and modified them for the 215 V8. The car runs a performer intake manifold and a carter 500CFM comp series carb. The coolant tubes go up through the heater tunnel in the corvair and the air is drawn through under the front bumper. At a look it's a stock corvair. Even the dual exhausts exit in a factory fashion. The rear seat was remove and a delete panel was put in place carpeted of course. There's one switch ont he dash to control twin electric fans on the front mounted rad. <P>The suspension has Koni's all around and heavy sway bars. The rear suspension has aluminum rods with spherical ends to stop any camber changes that may occur under heavy loads. The engine pulls hard from 2000 to 7000 with no flat spots. Hopefully we will get fuel injection on it soon. going down the highway it's hard to tell it's not an original corvair , it rides really well and is quite quiet until you get on it. We use it for autocrosses and the occasional show. It scored a best of 95.6pts out of 100 at the last national corvair convention it was at.<P>The car runs the 66 4spd tranny with 4spider gears , close ratio setup with 3.27 ratio. We had a 3.55 in it , but with all the torque you could start off in 2nd gear with no drama. Were considering a 5spd conversion that s now available<P>Fun car. Right now the engine displaces somewhere around 4.2ltrs, forged pistons , titanium valve springs , you name it , it's done. Unfortunatley it has eaten a cam shaft , were waiting for Lunati to get us a new one. Reverse grind cams are hard to come across. The car was completley dissassembled and sandblasted top to bottom , repainted and reassembled. The car was originally a 180 Turbo Corsa ..Canadian built  making it even rarer. (I'm sure some purists will disagree) We got the car in 1978 from the local wrecker for $50 ..yes $50. It had no rust , but the motor was shot , someone had stollen the turbo setup. The turbo motor as it came from GM still sits in my garage , untouched since the factory.<P>Thats the story of the V8 Corvair. I think it's my favorite car of the entire collection.<P>JM <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||watts||12-19-2001||12:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>The car runs a performer intake manifold and a carter 500CFM comp series carb. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Boy have we ever hijacked the original thread!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>How did you get the 500 comp to run lean enough?  The TR7 you see up above a few messages uses the same carb, but even with the leanest jets we could find it still runs so rich that we're getting terminal cylinder wash!  <P>Do you know what numbered jets you're using?<P>Thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Slammed Fiero||12-19-2001||12:44 AM||||Long story short.<P>Original carb a holley 390 double pumper , didn't work as well on the straights , but in the corners it wouldn't bog out like the carter would.<P>Rebuilt motor , holley 390 didn't want to idle , so we slapped ont he carter. It seems to run fine with the new motor , keep in mind this has been bored and stroked to 260 some odd cubes.<P>I will ask my dad about the carb ..<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000020||LAMBO||12-19-2001||09:08 AM||||Thanks Slammed for the info. It's definately given me inspiration to get back into a 'vair real soon. I've seen an iron block Buick v6 done this way in a corvair, but at the time, didn't ask any questions as to the "how". Maybe I'll see you with yours at a show sometime.<BR>TL<P>p.s. I got a copy of the article, by the way. Thanks to those that offered.<p>[This message has been edited by LAMBO (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000021||Slammed Fiero||12-19-2001||12:13 PM||||may I suggest the "Fastvair" list on yahoogroups...<P>Good stuff , there serious about their performance , no fluff , no sex in a corvair threads , or other BS.<P>The reason I have decided to go with a cheaper engine swap in the Fiero is because I want a Vair. Soooo much fun to drive when setup right. <P>JM||||reg||1
